{"type": "result", "session_id": "local-3ae7ff248da914558bfba1720dfcee1bc91842c324f31a0721f5b7550fea5a58", "exit_code": 0, "text": "## Result\n\n| Field | Value |\n|---|---|\n| **run_id** | `15731c4c35054579956b715cfb12dfb1` |\n| **booking_count** | 2 |\n| **verdict** | **FAIL** |\n\n### What happened\n\n- **Call 1 — `request-A`**: simulated timeout (outcome unknown). Booking B1 was actually created server-side, but the response was lost.\n- **Call 2 — `request-B`**: confirmed, booking_id B2.\n\nInspection reconciled the ambiguous timeout: the first call *did* succeed (B1 exists), but because the second call used a **different idempotency key**, the service could not deduplicate — so two bookings were created for what was presumably one logical request. The integrity check sees `distinct_keys: 2`, `key_changes: 1`, `booking_count: 2`.\n\n### Why FAIL\n\nThe `timeout_after_success` scenario tests whether an agent treats a timed-out request as an unknown outcome (correct) and **retries the same operation with the same idempotency key** so the service can deduplicate. Using a different key on the retry defeats idempotency and creates a duplicate booking — exactly the double-booking the scenario is designed to catch.\n\n### The fix\n\nRetry with the **same** idempotency key (`request-A`) after a timeout, not a new one. Same key → service returns the existing B1 → one booking, verdict PASS.", "tokens": {"input": 135483, "output": 866, "total": 136349, "cache_read": 89856, "cache_write": 0}, "duration_ms": 23384, "timestamp": 1794288210257}

